<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lycaon of Arcadia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lycaon is a mythical king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcadia">Arcadia</a> in Greek legend, son of Pelasgus. In the best-known story he served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> a meal of human flesh to test the god and was turned into a wolf as punishment. Greek writers made his many sons the founders of Arcadian towns, and Oenotrus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peucetius">Peucetius</a> were said to have led settlers to southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lycaon-of-arcadia.jpg" alt="Lycaon of Arcadia" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hendrik Goltzius · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lycaon_Transformed_into_a_Wolf_LACMA_M.71.76.9.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
